Z||000016||Archie||10-28-2000||03:17 PM||||The 3950 is for the White GT in the background.<P>I love the GT40, I've always liked that car. I've seen several at shows and while it is a pretty neat package the negative is that it is not a very accurate replica when compared to the GT40's on custom chassis'. But, I've never been one who has to have an exact replica, thus I like that car.<P>I've looked at putting the Small Block Ford engines into Fieros but have not done it. As it is right now, I have a V-8 kit that will allow the use of virtually any Small Block Chevy engine into a Fiero. Thru the years the SBC engines have changed very little and thus , with very few variations my one kit will fit ALL SBC engines. The same can't be said for the Ford engines. Thru the years Ford changed major features of the engines many times. One time I figured that if someone was to do a kit for Ford engines, that would allow the use of virtually any Small Block Ford engine, he would have to have 11 major variations of that kit. That means that it would be difficult to keep parts designed and in stock for whatever engine someone was getting ready to install. <P>Another reason is that I've spent my life working on Chevy engines & I feel that I can help a customer with about anything he encounters with a Chevy engine. I don't feel that confident about Fords. <P>Although you didn't ask, the above reasons are the same reasons I've never done a kit for the N-Star engines. <P>I feel that if a person is going to sell a kit to mount "such&such" engine in a Fiero then he should be an EXPERT on that swap. I feel that that person should be commited to that swap and convinced that there is no swap that is better for his customers' cars. I feel hat if a manufacturer is going to produce and sell a swap kit, then he should know ALL the "in's & out's" of that kit. A customer who puts down good money for an engine swap kit has every right to believe that the producer of that kit has all the answers. What concerns me is the manufacturer that has done only a few of his swaps and doesn't have all the answers when his customer runs into something the manufacturer hasn't seen yet. Also concerning me is the supplier that will sell you a 3.1, & 3.4 & 3.8 & other kits (a "whatever you want" type seller) he's not commited to a particular swap, how can he expect you to be.<P>Jumping off soapbox now.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000017||stimpy||10-28-2000||03:48 PM||||Makes sense to me... Do one thing and do it well.  I would still rather have a SC 3800 then a V8, but there is really no one who has established themselves in the field of that swap like Archie has with the SBC.  And has ayone else noticed that Archie is becoming less and less of a grumpy old fart?  Perhaps a kinder, gentler Archie? What's up with that? ||||reg||1
